import{at as e,it as t,n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.D9OOHiq-.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Attributes and Skills","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/modules/accessory/integration.md","filePath":"en/modules/accessory/integration.md","lastUpdated":0}`),a={name:`en/modules/accessory/integration.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="attributes-and-skills" tabindex="-1">Attributes and Skills <a class="header-anchor" href="#attributes-and-skills" aria-label="Permalink to “Attributes and Skills”">​</a></h1><p>Accessory attributes and skills reach EmakiAttribute and EmakiSkills; condition and durability decide whether an accessory contributes, and not every item can be placed in the accessory panel. Accessory attributes and skills are not computed by EmakiAccessory itself; they are registered into the public extension points of EmakiAttribute and EmakiSkills. Both are optional dependencies: when one is absent the corresponding half switches off while the accessory panel keeps working</p><h2 id="attributes-into-emakiattribute" tabindex="-1">Attributes into EmakiAttribute <a class="header-anchor" href="#attributes-into-emakiattribute" aria-label="Permalink to “Attributes into EmakiAttribute”">​</a></h2><p>EmakiAccessory registers a <strong>contribution provider</strong> with EmakiAttribute (id <code>emakiaccessory:accessories</code>, priority 100) to merge data into attribute computation: accessory attributes are fed in as a distinct source into the combat attribute snapshot. The provider registers no custom equipment slot and writes nothing onto the player&#39;s vanilla attributes</p><p>The reported source ids:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Source id</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>emakiaccessory:accessory</code></td><td>Attributes of an individual accessory</td></tr><tr><td><code>emakiaccessory:set</code></td><td>Accessory set bonuses</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Attribute values are read through EmakiAttribute&#39;s <code>AttributeCatalog#itemSnapshot</code> — <strong>the same parser the equipment path uses</strong>. An accessory and a helmet carrying identical data therefore produce identical numbers, and parent-attribute expansion plus range-spread companion keys come along with it</p><p>Range-spread companion keys are summed like any other key; otherwise a ranged accessory would keep its lower bound and lose its spread</p><p>When EmakiAttribute is unavailable (<code>EmakiAttributeApi.status().usable()</code> is false) no provider is registered, so accessories grant no attributes at all</p><h2 id="skills-into-emakiskills" tabindex="-1">Skills into EmakiSkills <a class="header-anchor" href="#skills-into-emakiskills" aria-label="Permalink to “Skills into EmakiSkills”">​</a></h2><p>EmakiAccessory registers a <strong>skill source</strong> with EmakiSkills (provider id <code>accessories</code>). Equipment skill payloads on an accessory are read by <code>AccessorySkillPayloadCodec.read</code>, the same PDC contract Item, Forge, Gem and Strengthen write</p><p>Skills listed in an accessory set&#39;s <code>thresholds[].effects[].es_skills</code> take effect through that same path</p><p>The source-slot field of each skill entry is the <strong>slot instance id</strong> for single-accessory skills and <code>set:&lt;setId&gt;</code> for set skills</p><p>With EmakiSkills absent the module still enables normally: <code>AccessoryProviderRegistrar</code> loads the integration class reflectively by name (<code>emaki.jiuwu.craft.accessory.provider.AccessorySkillsProviderIntegration</code>) and only does so while <code>EmakiSkills</code> is enabled, so a missing EmakiSkills cannot fail class loading — there is simply no skill source</p><h2 id="slot-restriction-criterion" tabindex="-1">Slot Restriction Criterion <a class="header-anchor" href="#slot-restriction-criterion" aria-label="Permalink to “Slot Restriction Criterion”">​</a></h2><p>The accessory window accepts <strong>only items that explicitly declare accessory slots</strong>. There is exactly one item-carried declaration source:</p><ol><li><code>type: &quot;accessory_slot&quot;</code> inside an EmakiItem definition&#39;s <code>effects</code>, written by EmakiItem into item PDC under <code>emakiitem:accessory_slots</code> (string list)</li></ol><p>On top of that, <code>slot_sources.pdc</code> and <code>slot_sources.lore</code> in <code>config.yml</code> are additional sources that operations staff opt into explicitly; both default to disabled. When enabled they are judged together with the item-carried declaration, and a hit on any one of them passes</p><p>The <code>active_slot</code> metadata of EmakiAttribute payloads and the EmakiSkills payload active_slot <strong>no longer</strong> take part in accessory matching. Under the old behaviour an unspecified slot was written as <code>all</code>, which let every EmakiItem item enter any accessory cell; such items are now rejected for having no <code>accessory_slots</code> declaration. Attribute and skill contributions use the same criterion, so for a cell whose declaration does not match, the item is refused and no contribution is produced</p><p>The matching rule: the item passes as soon as <strong>any single declaration hits the current cell</strong> (any-of)</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>The item&#39;s declared slot</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A part id, such as <code>ring</code></td><td>Active in any cell of that part</td></tr><tr><td>A slot instance id, such as <code>ring_1</code></td><td>Active only in that cell</td></tr><tr><td>Anything else (such as the equipment slot names <code>hand</code> or <code>main_hand</code>)</td><td>Hits no accessory slot, i.e. no declaration</td></tr><tr><td>No <code>accessory_slots</code> key (and no optional source matched either)</td><td>Placement rejected with <code>gui.not_declared</code>, and the contribution is dropped entirely</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two container-level safeguards back this up:</p><ul><li>A failing criterion at placement time rejects the item, leaving the cursor stack untouched on the cursor so it never enters the cell</li><li>Closing the window runs a validation pass over <strong>every page</strong>, taking items that are no longer inside their slot&#39;s declaration and returning them to the player&#39;s inventory (dropped at their feet when the inventory is full), with one <code>gui.invalid_returned</code> message per item. Orphan cells (slots the page no longer declares) are unaffected and keep their own retrieval flow</li></ul><p>If the equipment-side rules ever change, this accessory-side implementation must be re-evaluated</p><h2 id="condition-gating" tabindex="-1">Condition Gating <a class="header-anchor" href="#condition-gating" aria-label="Permalink to “Condition Gating”">​</a></h2><p>Accessories in the window are checked against their own definition&#39;s <code>condition</code>: when it fails, that accessory&#39;s attributes, skills and set piece count <strong>all</strong> stop contributing, while the item stays in its cell</p><p>The check runs through EmakiItemApi&#39;s silent query <code>ItemCatalog#conditionSatisfied(player, itemStack)</code> — the silent counterpart of <code>conditionPasses</code>: it never runs <code>on_pass</code>/<code>on_fail</code> actions and never sends <code>on_fail.message</code>, so it is safe to evaluate repeatedly during recomputation and rendering. The trigger context matches the equipment contribution gate: <code>contribution</code></p><p>Evaluation happens on <strong>contribution recomputation</strong>: GUI edits, page switches, a completed data load, reload, <code>/eacc retrieve</code>, death drops, and durability break. A change to the condition itself (level, class, placeholders) therefore reaches the accessory window only on the next recompute — unlike the equipment path, which evaluates on every collection</p><p>When EmakiItem is absent, the player is offline, the call is off the player&#39;s owner thread, or the definition cannot be resolved, the query fails and the accessory side treats the accessory as satisfied (no gating), so a missing dependency never silently disables accessories</p><p>In the window, an accessory whose condition fails shows one extra <code>gui.condition_unmet</code> line; that line only exists in the rendered projection and is never written back to the item</p><h2 id="durability-gating" tabindex="-1">Durability Gating <a class="header-anchor" href="#durability-gating" aria-label="Permalink to “Durability Gating”">​</a></h2><p>With <code>durability.enabled</code> turned on, accessories on the enabled page lose durability on player damage according to <code>durability.damage_per_hit</code>; once an accessory is exhausted its attributes, skills and set piece count all stop applying, the item stays in its cell and shows a <code>gui.durability_broken</code> marker. See <a href="./configuration.html">main configuration</a> and <a href="./parts.html">part definitions</a> for the criterion and configuration details</p><h2 id="when-recomputation-happens" tabindex="-1">When Recomputation Happens <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-recomputation-happens" aria-label="Permalink to “When Recomputation Happens”">​</a></h2><p>Contribution snapshots are recomputed when accessory contents change: a GUI edit, a completed data load, a reload, a <code>/eacc retrieve</code>, or a death drop that clears the enabled page. Opening the window without changing data does not trigger a recompute. Both the condition and durability gates are evaluated on this path</p><p>Recomputation deliberately <strong>does not happen inside the provider callback</strong>. EmakiAttribute&#39;s combat snapshot collector calls the contribution provider while computing its cache signature, meaning the provider runs on every combat snapshot read; parsing PDC there would put item deserialisation on the combat hot path</p><h2 id="threading" tabindex="-1">Threading <a class="header-anchor" href="#threading" aria-label="Permalink to “Threading”">​</a></h2><p>Recomputation must run on the owner thread of the player holding the items: the attribute parser writes its result back into item PDC as a cache, so it is not safe off-thread. The condition query has the same requirement, and a wrong-thread call counts as satisfied</p><h2 id="behaviour-with-modules-missing" tabindex="-1">Behaviour With Modules Missing <a class="header-anchor" href="#behaviour-with-modules-missing" aria-label="Permalink to “Behaviour With Modules Missing”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Missing module</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>EmakiAttribute</td><td>No attribute provider is registered, so accessories grant no attributes; the panel and set piece counting keep working (set counting depends on EmakiItem only)</td></tr><tr><td>EmakiSkills</td><td>No skill source is registered, so neither accessory nor set skills are granted; everything else keeps working</td></tr><tr><td>EmakiItem</td><td>Items carry no <code>accessory_slots</code> declaration any more (the key already present on existing items still counts), so nothing can be placed unless a <code>slot_sources</code> source matches; condition gating is skipped entirely (treated as satisfied); <code>unique</code> deduplication falls back to vanilla material matching and stays active; accessory sets relying on <code>pieces[].item</code> are entirely inactive (their piece count stays 0)</td></tr><tr><td>PlaceholderAPI</td><td>PAPI placeholders in text are no longer replaced and pass through unchanged</td></tr></tbody></table><p>EmakiAccessory itself has <strong>no</strong> PlaceholderAPI expansion and exposes no <code>%emakiaccessory_*%</code> placeholders; it only consumes PlaceholderAPI optionally. The module also registers <strong>no</strong> actions into the CoreLib action registry (the module&#39;s only <code>super(&quot;accessory&quot;, …)</code> is the config-precheck contributor&#39;s module id, not an action segment)</p>`,40)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};